From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 19:57:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 60/64] fix(gastown): claim the polecat's WORK bead for the
 whole run (gcp-5gir)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mol-polecat-work claimed only its own STEP beads. The WORK bead stayed
`open` and unassigned from dispatch until submit-and-exit handed it to the
refinery, so for the entire implementation window it was indistinguishable,
in `gc bd ready`, from a bead nobody had touched. Any seat that surveys ready
work — the mayor, a backlog groomer, another crew seat — could `gc sling` a
second polecat onto a bead already being written, and per the polecat contract
the later push supersedes: work already implemented and self-reviewed is
discarded. Observed live on winnow-x32.4/.5 and winnow-48fm, and reproduced
first-party on this rig by the sling that dispatched gcp-5gir itself.

workspace-setup now claims the work bead in step 1, before the worktree is
built: status=in_progress, assignee=this session, and polecat_session stamped
alongside the assignee so the two can never disagree. The write is read back
(and retried) because `--status` on this bead has been seen not to stick
(gcp-s14g), and a status that stays `open` leaves the re-sling window open with
the fix reading as applied. A work bead already held by somebody else — the
refinery, or an operator escalation — is left alone rather than stolen.
submit-and-exit is the single release point and already moves the assignee
straight to the refinery, so ownership never passes back through unassigned.

Holding the claim has two consequences, and both are handled here rather than
left to be discovered in production:

- `gc hook --claim`'s crash-recovery tier is
  `bd list --status in_progress --assignee=<you> --limit=1`. With the work bead
  claimed it matches, and at every step boundary — successor step still stored
  `open` — it is the ONLY match, so the hook hands back the work bead instead
  of the next formula step. That skips self-review and submit-and-exit and the
  branch is never pushed; it is the same shape as gcp-tl8, where a refinery
  resumed a held work bead as its patrol wisp on every restart. The polecat's
  startup claim block now re-points at the molecule's next ready step whenever
  the hook returns a bead carrying no `gc.step_ref`, printing STEP_REPOINTED.
- The done sequence's already-submitted guard keyed on "assigned to somebody
  who is not this session". A pool restart mints a new session identity, so a
  resumed molecule legitimately finds its bead held by its own PREVIOUS
  session; without a correction that reads as a completed handoff and drains
  with the branch unpushed. Both copies of the guard (polecat prompt and the
  approval-fallacy fragment) now exclude an assignee that matches
  metadata.polecat_session.

Deliberately not done: stamping gc.routed_to on the work bead. Pool dispatch
leaves it blank there on purpose and carries routing on the molecule root,
which is what lets scale_check see pool demand.

Validation: new contract test test_work_bead_is_claimed_for_the_whole_run in
gastown/tests/test_gastown_pack_assets.sh, verified red before green against
three separate reversions (claim removed, re-point marker removed, session-tag
exclusion removed). Full gastown shell suite, `python3 -m pytest tests`,
go vet/build/test, golangci-lint and validate_registry all pass. The two
gascity claim-command subprocess timeouts are pre-existing on origin/integration
(gcp-p38x, gcp-135m) and were reproduced there from a clean worktree.
